message.reasoning_content:
We need to design a PLC program (structured text) that controls a mechanical arm with six basic actions (down, up, left, right, grip, release). Modes: manual, single step, single cycle, continuous. Mode selection inputs are mutually exclusive (only one true at a time). Emergency stop can stop all actions at any time.

We need to provide a complete ST implementation (Structured Text) for a PLC program. The interface is defined: inputs and outputs as booleans.

We need to ensure we follow the requirements exactly: manual mode: each action controlled individually via control panel, if not at position (limit switch false) and button pressed, then command true; if at position (limit switch true) then command false; release stops movement. So we need to check DownButton and DownLimitSwitch etc.

In single step mode: the mechanical arm automatically executes one action per start button press according to preset program. The program: sequence: down, grip, up, right, down, release, up, left, then repeat. Starting at top left, so initial position: up and left limit switches are true? Actually initial position at top left means it's at up position and left position simultaneously. So UpLimitSwitch=1 and LeftLimitSwitch=1. The other limit switches (DownLimitSwitch, RightLimitSwitch, GripLimitSwitch, ReleaseLimitSwitch) are false initially (except maybe grip limit switch is false, release limit switch false). In single step mode, each press of start button triggers the next action. The action should execute automatically until its limit switch indicates completion. Then stop. Then wait for next start.

We need a state machine that tracks current step in the sequence for single step mode.

In single cycle mode: when start pressed, automatically runs through entire sequence without requiring further start presses. After completing, stops at final position (top left) and waits for next start.

In continuous mode: same as single cycle but repeats continuously while start is held or until emergency stop? The spec says: when start button is pressed, the mechanical arm continuously executes the handling cycle of the single cycle mode. So we can treat it as: start triggers continuous execution; maybe keep running until stop or emergency stop. Since there is no stop button defined, maybe continuous mode runs until emergency stop triggered, or maybe start is a toggle? The spec: "when the start button is pressed, the mechanical arm continuously executes the handling cycle of the single cycle mode." Could interpret that start triggers continuous operation, and pressing start again does nothing (or maybe toggles). Usually continuous mode runs continuously after start press, and may be stopped by emergency stop only, or maybe by pressing start again toggles off. But the spec doesn't mention a stop button. So we could implement that continuous mode runs as long as start is true (i.e., start is held). But the description says "when the start button is pressed, the mechanical arm continuously executes..." That might mean start press (rising edge) initiates continuous operation, and it continues indefinitely (or until emergency stop). Since we don't have stop, we will use a toggle: start press toggles continuous operation on/off. But to keep simple, we can make continuous mode run only while start is true; if start becomes false, it stops after finishing the current cycle? Or we could treat start as a start command that initiates continuous cycles until emergency stop. But to be safe, we can implement continuous mode as: when start is true and not already in continuous run, start continuous mode; then keep cycling until start goes false or emergency stop. However, they didn't ask for stop button; but they might want the start button to act as a start for continuous mode and emergency stop can stop all. We'll implement as: continuous mode runs continuously after start pressed (rising edge) and continues until emergency stop triggers (or maybe until start is pressed again). We'll incorporate a flag "ContinuousRunning" that toggles on rising edge of start.

Given that manual mode is mutually exclusive with other modes, we must ensure that when ManualMode is true, all other mode logic is disabled.

Emergency stop: if EmergencyStop is false (meaning triggered), all outputs must be false (stop all actions). Also should reset any running state? The spec says "emergency stop function can stop all actions of the mechanical arm at any time." So we must set all output commands to false when EmergencyStop = FALSE.

Also need to consider that limit switches indicate position. For movement commands (MoveDown, MoveUp, MoveLeft, MoveRight), we need to keep the command true while not at target limit and stop when limit reached. For grip and release, we need to keep the command true until GripLimitSwitch or ReleaseLimitSwitch is true.

But there might be nuance: In manual mode, if limit switch is already true (i.e., at that position), then pressing the button for that direction should not cause movement. Also, releasing the button stops the movement (i.e., command goes false). So we need to implement something like: MoveDown := ManualMode AND DownButton AND NOT DownLimitSwitch AND EmergencyStop.

Similarly for others: MoveUp := ManualMode AND UpButton AND NOT UpLimitSwitch; MoveLeft := ManualMode AND LeftButton AND NOT LeftLimitSwitch; MoveRight := ManualMode AND RightButton AND NOT RightLimitSwitch; ActivateGrip := ManualMode AND GripButton AND NOT GripLimitSwitch; ActivateRelease := ManualMode AND ReleaseButton AND NOT ReleaseLimitSwitch.

Make sure that all commands are off when EmergencyStop is false.

Now for other modes: We'll need to manage a state machine.

Define an enumeration or integer variable "CurrentStep" representing the step in the sequence for single step and single cycle/continuous. Steps 0-7 (maybe 8 steps). We'll define:

0: MoveDown (down)
1: ActivateGrip (grip)
2: MoveUp (up)
3: MoveRight (right)
4: MoveDown (down)
5: ActivateRelease (release)
6: MoveUp (up)
7: MoveLeft (left)

Then after step 7, we go back to step 0 for continuous mode; for single cycle mode we stop after step 7 (i.e., remain at step 7) and wait for next start; for single step mode we also stop after each step and wait for next start.

We need to detect when the current action is completed. For movement actions, the completion condition is the limit switch for the target position (e.g., MoveDown completes when DownLimitSwitch = TRUE). For ActivateGrip, completion when GripLimitSwitch = TRUE. For ActivateRelease, completion when ReleaseLimitSwitch = TRUE.

We also need to ensure that the arm starts at initial position: UpLimitSwitch = TRUE and LeftLimitSwitch = TRUE. So the first movement down will be from that position.

Implementation details: We'll have a variable "StepIndex" (INT) to hold current step. Also "StepDone" flag to indicate the current step's action has completed. "StartPressedEdge" detection for start button (rising edge). We'll need to differentiate between modes.

We need to handle start button press differently for each mode:

- Manual mode: start button is not used for controlling movement; manual mode uses its own buttons. Start may be ignored.

- Single step mode: start press advances to next step and starts the action. But we need to detect rising edge of Start button only when SingleStepMode is true.

- Single cycle mode: start press triggers the entire cycle. We need to detect rising edge of Start button when SingleCycleMode is true. Then we start at step 0 and proceed continuously through all steps without waiting for further start presses. After finishing step 7, we stop (remain at step 7). The cycle may be repeated only when start pressed again.

- Continuous mode: start press toggles continuous operation. So we detect rising edge of Start when ContinuousMode is true. Then set a flag "ContinuousActive". While ContinuousActive = true, after completing step 7 we automatically loop back to step 0 and continue. If EmergencyStop triggers, we stop all and maybe reset ContinuousActive.

Alternatively, we can treat continuous mode as while Start is true (i.e., as long as start button is held) the arm cycles. But that may not match "when start button is pressed" (once) triggers continuous execution.

Given the description: "Continuous mode: the initial position of the mechanical arm is at the top left, when the start button is pressed, the mechanical arm continuously executes the handling cycle of the single cycle mode." It doesn't mention stopping. So we assume it continues until emergency stop. So we can implement continuous mode such that once start is pressed (rising edge) it sets a flag that enables continuous cycling. It stops only on emergency stop. We'll also need a way to start continuous mode after emergency stop resets (EmergencyStop becomes true). So we can define a variable "ContinuousRunning". When start rising edge and ContinuousMode is true, set ContinuousRunning := TRUE. When EmergencyStop = FALSE, we should set ContinuousRunning := FALSE and also set all outputs false. When EmergencyStop becomes true again after being false, we might want to resume? The spec says emergency stop can stop all actions at any time. It doesn't say about resuming after emergency stop. We'll treat emergency stop as a safety that stops all outputs and resets the state machine to idle (maybe step index to 0). Then after emergency stop is cleared, we need to press start again to resume.

We'll also need to track the current action command based on step index.

We'll also need to differentiate between continuous mode and single cycle mode: in single cycle mode, after finishing step 7 we set a flag "CycleComplete" and stop.

In single step mode, after finishing a step, we set "StepComplete" and wait for next start press. The step index remains at the completed step? Actually after completing a step, we need to advance to next step on next start press. So we keep step index at current step (the one that just completed). On next start press, we increment step index (mod 8) and start next action.

But the description says: "When the start button is pressed once, the mechanical arm executes the down command MoveDown, and stops after reaching the down position. When the start button is pressed again, the mechanical arm executes the grip command ActivateGrip, and stops after gripping." So after each action is completed, we wait for next start. So we need to have a state that indicates we are waiting for start after completing an action. So we can have a state "Idle" where we wait for start press. Then on start press we start the action for current step index. After action completes, we go back to Idle. The step index increments only on start press. That fits.

For single cycle mode, we start at step 0 on start press, then we continuously run through steps without waiting for start press. So we need a state "RunningCycle" that goes through steps automatically.

For continuous mode, we also start at step 0 on start press, then continuously cycle until emergency stop.

Thus we need a state machine with states: IDLE, RUN_STEP, STEP_COMPLETE (or WAIT_FOR_START), RUN_CYCLE, etc.

But we can implement simpler: Have a variable "ModeState" indicating the current mode (manual, idle, running). For single step mode, we have "StepIndex" and a flag "StepRunning". When StepRunning = FALSE, we are waiting for start. On rising edge of Start and SingleStepMode = TRUE and not StepRunning, we set StepRunning := TRUE and start command for current step. Then we monitor completion. When completion condition is true, we set StepRunning := FALSE, and increment StepIndex (mod 8). Then loop.

For single cycle mode: On rising edge of Start and SingleCycleMode = TRUE, we set StepIndex := 0 and StepRunning := TRUE, and start command for step 0. Then we monitor completion, when step completes, we increment StepIndex, start next step, until StepIndex reaches 8 (or after step 7 completes). At that point we set StepRunning := FALSE and maybe set a flag CycleComplete. The cycle stops.

For continuous mode: similar to single cycle but after step 7 completes we wrap StepIndex to 0 and continue.

We also need to handle emergency stop: if EmergencyStop = FALSE, then we set all outputs false, set StepRunning = FALSE, maybe also reset StepIndex to 0? The spec doesn't specify resetting, but it's safe to reset to initial state. We'll do that.

We also need to ensure that limit switches are used correctly: For movement commands, we need to keep command true until limit switch is true. But we also need to ensure that we don't start moving in a direction if already at that limit (i.e., limit switch already true). In the step logic, we will only start a movement if the target limit is false (i.e., not at target). That is typical. For grip/release, we also only start if limit is false.

Thus we can implement a function or set of IF statements for each step.

We also need to handle rising edge detection for Start button. Since we have a PLC program in ST, we can use a variable "StartPrev" to store previous state and detect rising edge.

We also need to handle the case where start is pressed while an action is already running (maybe ignore). In single step mode, we should ignore start while StepRunning = TRUE. In single cycle mode, we should ignore start while StepRunning = TRUE (i.e., cycle already running). In continuous mode, same.

Now, we need to produce a complete ST implementation. The spec expects a single program that uses the defined inputs and outputs. We'll produce code with proper variable declarations, maybe using global variables or directly mapping to inputs/outputs.

We can define:

VAR_INPUT
    ManualMode : BOOL;
    SingleStepMode : BOOL;
    SingleCycleMode : BOOL;
    ContinuousMode : BOOL;
    EmergencyStop : BOOL;
    Start : BOOL;
    DownButton : BOOL;
    UpButton : BOOL;
    LeftButton : BOOL;
    RightButton : BOOL;
    GripButton : BOOL;
    ReleaseButton : BOOL;
    DownLimitSwitch : BOOL;
    UpLimitSwitch : BOOL;
    LeftLimitSwitch : BOOL;
    RightLimitSwitch : BOOL;
    GripLimitSwitch : BOOL;
    ReleaseLimitSwitch : BOOL;
END_VAR

VAR_OUTPUT
    MoveDown : BOOL;
    MoveUp : BOOL;
    MoveLeft : BOOL;
    MoveRight : BOOL;
    ActivateGrip : BOOL;
    ActivateRelease : BOOL;
END_VAR

VAR
    StepIndex : INT := 0;
    StepRunning : BOOL := FALSE;
    CycleComplete : BOOL := FALSE;
    ContinuousRunning : BOOL := FALSE;
    StartPrev : BOOL := FALSE;
    // maybe state for mode handling
    Mode : INT; // 0 = manual, 1 = single step, 2 = single cycle, 3 = continuous
END_VAR

But we can also derive Mode from the inputs: we could have a variable "CurrentMode" set based on which mode input is true. But we can also just use the inputs directly in the logic: e.g., if ManualMode then handle manual; elsif SingleStepMode then handle single step; etc. Since they are mutually exclusive, we can use IF..ELSIF.

We also need to manage the state for each mode.

We also need to handle the case where we need to detect rising edge of Start only when not in manual mode. We'll have a variable "StartRisingEdge" defined as (Start AND NOT StartPrev). Then we set StartPrev := Start.

Now we need to implement each mode's logic.

We'll also need to handle the "EmergencyStop" input: The spec says "FALSE: Emergency stop triggered, TRUE: No emergency stop triggered". So if EmergencyStop = FALSE, we must set all outputs false and maybe reset StepRunning and other states. So we will check at the top: if NOT EmergencyStop then set all outputs false, set StepRunning := FALSE, ContinuousRunning := FALSE, and maybe set StepIndex := 0, and exit (or skip other logic). This ensures emergency stop overrides everything.

Thus we can structure:

IF NOT EmergencyStop THEN
    MoveDown := FALSE;
    MoveUp := FALSE;
    MoveLeft := FALSE;
    MoveRight := FALSE;
    ActivateGrip := FALSE;
    ActivateRelease := FALSE;
    StepRunning := FALSE;
    ContinuousRunning := FALSE;
    // maybe also reset StepIndex to 0
    StepIndex := 0;
    // ensure we don't process other modes
    RETURN;
END_IF

Now if EmergencyStop is true (no emergency), we proceed.

We also need to handle the case where we are in manual mode. If ManualMode is true, we set all other mode logic to be ignored. So we can check if ManualMode then handle manual and skip other logic.

Thus:

IF ManualMode THEN
    // manual logic
    MoveDown := DownButton AND NOT DownLimitSwitch;
    MoveUp := UpButton AND NOT UpLimitSwitch;
    MoveLeft := LeftButton AND NOT LeftLimitSwitch;
    MoveRight := RightButton AND NOT RightLimitSwitch;
    ActivateGrip := GripButton AND NOT GripLimitSwitch;
    ActivateRelease := ReleaseButton AND NOT ReleaseLimitSwitch;
    // ensure other outputs false
    // also ensure step states reset
    StepRunning := FALSE;
    ContinuousRunning := FALSE;
    StepIndex := 0;
    RETURN;
END_IF

Now we have other modes: SingleStepMode, SingleCycleMode, ContinuousMode.

We need to handle rising edge detection for Start. We'll compute StartRisingEdge each cycle.

Now we need to manage step logic for each mode.

We can implement a function or subprogram that runs the step logic based on StepIndex and StepRunning.

We also need to consider that limit switches may be true initially (like UpLimitSwitch and LeftLimitSwitch). For movement steps, we need to ensure we only start moving if the limit for the target position is not already true. But we also need to ensure we don't get stuck if we are already at that position at the start of the step. For example, after completing step 0 (down), we will have DownLimitSwitch = TRUE. Then we want to go to step 1 (grip). That is fine. After step 1 (grip) we will move up; UpLimitSwitch is initially true, but after moving down, we are at down position, so UpLimitSwitch is false. So we need to move up. So we need to check target limit before moving. So we need to set MoveUp command only if UpLimitSwitch = FALSE. This is typical.

Similarly for left and right: after moving right, RightLimitSwitch = TRUE, then moving down, then release, then up, then left: LeftLimitSwitch is initially true but after moving right and down and up again, we need to move left back to initial position. At that point LeftLimitSwitch may be false (if we are at rightmost position). So we will check.

Thus we can implement step logic like:

CASE StepIndex OF
0: // MoveDown
    IF NOT StepRunning THEN
        // start step if target not reached
        IF NOT DownLimitSwitch THEN
            MoveDown := TRUE;
            StepRunning := TRUE;
        ELSE
            // already at down position, skip to next step
            StepRunning := FALSE;
            StepIndex := 1;
        END_IF;
    ELSE
        // if step running, keep command true until limit reached
        MoveDown := TRUE;
        IF DownLimitSwitch THEN
            MoveDown := FALSE;
            StepRunning := FALSE;
            StepIndex := 1;
        END_IF;
    END_IF;
1: // ActivateGrip
    IF NOT StepRunning THEN
        IF NOT GripLimitSwitch THEN
            ActivateGrip := TRUE;
            StepRunning := TRUE;
        ELSE
            StepRunning := FALSE;
            StepIndex := 2;
        END_IF;
    ELSE
        ActivateGrip := TRUE;
        IF GripLimitSwitch THEN
            ActivateGrip := FALSE;
            StepRunning := FALSE;
            StepIndex := 2;
        END_IF;
    END_IF;
2: // MoveUp
    IF NOT StepRunning THEN
        IF NOT UpLimitSwitch THEN
            MoveUp := TRUE;
            StepRunning := TRUE;
        ELSE
            StepRunning := FALSE;
            StepIndex := 3;
        END_IF;
    ELSE
        MoveUp := TRUE;
        IF UpLimitSwitch THEN
            MoveUp := FALSE;
            StepRunning := FALSE;
            StepIndex := 3;
        END_IF;
    END_IF;
3: // MoveRight
    IF NOT StepRunning THEN
        IF NOT RightLimitSwitch THEN
            MoveRight := TRUE;
            StepRunning := TRUE;
        ELSE
            StepRunning := FALSE;
            StepIndex := 4;
        END_IF;
    ELSE
        MoveRight := TRUE;
        IF RightLimitSwitch THEN
            MoveRight := FALSE;
            StepRunning := FALSE;
            StepIndex := 4;
        END_IF;
    END_IF;
4: // MoveDown
    IF NOT StepRunning THEN
        IF NOT DownLimitSwitch THEN
            MoveDown := TRUE;
            StepRunning := TRUE;
        ELSE
            StepRunning := FALSE;
            StepIndex := 5;
        END_IF;
    ELSE
        MoveDown := TRUE;
        IF DownLimitSwitch THEN
            MoveDown := FALSE;
            StepRunning := FALSE;
            StepIndex := 5;
        END_IF;
    END_IF;
5: // ActivateRelease
    IF NOT StepRunning THEN
        IF NOT ReleaseLimitSwitch THEN
            ActivateRelease := TRUE;
            StepRunning := TRUE;
        ELSE
            StepRunning := FALSE;
            StepIndex := 6;
        END_IF;
    ELSE
        ActivateRelease := TRUE;
        IF ReleaseLimitSwitch THEN
            ActivateRelease := FALSE;
            StepRunning := FALSE;
            StepIndex := 6;
        END_IF;
    END_IF;
6: // MoveUp
    IF NOT StepRunning THEN
        IF NOT UpLimitSwitch THEN
            MoveUp := TRUE;
            StepRunning := TRUE;
        ELSE
            StepRunning := FALSE;
            StepIndex := 7;
        END_IF;
    ELSE
        MoveUp := TRUE;
        IF UpLimitSwitch THEN
            MoveUp := FALSE;
            StepRunning := FALSE;
            StepIndex := 7;
        END_IF;
    END_IF;
7: // MoveLeft
    IF NOT StepRunning THEN
        IF NOT LeftLimitSwitch THEN
            MoveLeft := TRUE;
            StepRunning := TRUE;
        ELSE
            StepRunning := FALSE;
            StepIndex := 0; // for continuous mode loop, else will be set to 0 after cycle
        END_IF;
    ELSE
        MoveLeft := TRUE;
        IF LeftLimitSwitch THEN
            MoveLeft := FALSE;
            StepRunning := FALSE;
            // for single cycle mode, we stop here; for continuous we loop later
            StepIndex := 0;
        END_IF;
    END_IF;
END_CASE;

But we need to differentiate between modes. The above case will automatically loop step index from 7 to 0. But we need to handle mode-specific behavior: In single cycle mode, after step 7 completes, we should stop and not automatically start again. In continuous mode, we should continue looping. In single step mode, we should wait for start after each step.

Thus we need to incorporate mode handling. We'll have separate logic for each mode. The case block above can be used for step execution, but we need to control when to start and when to increment step index based on mode.

Simplify: We'll have separate state variables for each mode: e.g., SingleStepState (enum: Idle, Running), SingleCycleState (enum: Idle, Running, Done), ContinuousState (enum: Idle, Running). But we can also use a single set of variables (StepIndex, StepRunning) and differentiate by mode logic that triggers start.

Better approach: Use a mode variable "OperatingMode" that can be set to MANUAL, SINGLE_STEP, SINGLE_CYCLE, CONTINUOUS. Then we can handle each mode in separate IF branches.

Pseudo-code:

// Detect start rising edge
StartRisingEdge := Start AND NOT StartPrev;
StartPrev := Start;

// Emergency stop
IF NOT EmergencyStop THEN
    // all off
    MoveDown := FALSE; MoveUp := FALSE; MoveLeft := FALSE; MoveRight := FALSE;
    ActivateGrip := FALSE; ActivateRelease := FALSE;
    StepRunning := FALSE;
    ContinuousRunning := FALSE;
    StepIndex := 0;
    RETURN;
END_IF;

// Manual mode
IF ManualMode THEN
    MoveDown := DownButton AND NOT DownLimitSwitch;
    MoveUp := UpButton AND NOT UpLimitSwitch;
    MoveLeft := LeftButton AND NOT LeftLimitSwitch;
    MoveRight := RightButton AND NOT RightLimitSwitch;
    ActivateGrip := GripButton AND NOT GripLimitSwitch;
    ActivateRelease := ReleaseButton AND NOT ReleaseLimitSwitch;
    // Reset other states
    StepRunning := FALSE;
    ContinuousRunning := FALSE;
    StepIndex := 0;
    RETURN;
END_IF;

// For other modes, we need to handle start button.
// Determine which mode is active
IF SingleStepMode THEN
    // Single step mode logic
    IF StepRunning THEN
        // handle step completion
        // check completion based on StepIndex
        // if completed, set StepRunning := FALSE; // wait for next start
    ELSE
        // waiting for start
        IF StartRisingEdge THEN
            // start the current step (StepIndex)
            StepRunning := TRUE;
        END_IF;
    END_IF;
    // Execute command based on StepIndex and StepRunning
    // (like case statement)
ELSIF SingleCycleMode THEN
    // Single cycle mode logic
    IF StepRunning THEN
        // handle step completion, after step 7, set StepRunning := FALSE and set CycleComplete
    ELSE
        // waiting for start
        IF StartRisingEdge THEN
            StepIndex := 0;
            StepRunning := TRUE;
        END_IF;
    END_IF;
    // Execute command based on StepIndex and StepRunning
ELSIF ContinuousMode THEN
    // Continuous mode logic
    IF ContinuousRunning THEN
        // run cycles continuously; after step 7, loop back to step 0
        // If emergency stop, we already handled above.
    ELSE
        IF StartRisingEdge THEN
            StepIndex := 0;
            StepRunning := TRUE;
            ContinuousRunning := TRUE;
        END_IF;
    END_IF;
    // Execute command based on StepIndex and StepRunning
END_IF;

// In all modes except manual, we need to clear commands when not running or step not active.

Now we need to implement the command execution part. We'll have a separate function that sets the appropriate command based on StepIndex and StepRunning. For each step, we set the command true if StepRunning = TRUE and we are at that step. Also we need to handle completion detection within the same block.

Simplify: Instead of separate case for command and completion, we can combine them in one case block that runs each cycle. But we need to differentiate between modes for step advancement. However, we can handle step advancement in each mode's logic and then have a common case block for commands.

Better: We'll have a state machine for each mode. But we can also unify by using "StepRunning" to indicate that we are currently executing a step, and we will check completion within that block and then set StepRunning to FALSE accordingly. Then each mode's logic will decide whether to start next step automatically or wait for start.

Thus we can have:

// Common step execution
IF StepRunning THEN
    CASE StepIndex OF
    0: // MoveDown
        MoveDown := TRUE;
        IF DownLimitSwitch THEN
            MoveDown := FALSE;
            StepRunning := FALSE;
            // increment step index after step
            StepIndex := 1;
        END_IF;
    1: // ActivateGrip
        ActivateGrip := TRUE;
        IF GripLimitSwitch THEN
            ActivateGrip := FALSE;
            StepRunning := FALSE;
            StepIndex := 2;
        END_IF;
    2: // MoveUp
        MoveUp := TRUE;
        IF UpLimitSwitch THEN
            MoveUp := FALSE;
            StepRunning := FALSE;
            StepIndex := 3;
        END_IF;
    3: // MoveRight
        MoveRight := TRUE;
        IF RightLimitSwitch THEN
            MoveRight := FALSE;
            StepRunning := FALSE;
            StepIndex := 4;
        END_IF;
    4: // MoveDown
        MoveDown := TRUE;
        IF DownLimitSwitch THEN
            MoveDown := FALSE;
            StepRunning := FALSE;
            StepIndex := 5;
        END_IF;
    5: // ActivateRelease
        ActivateRelease := TRUE;
        IF ReleaseLimitSwitch THEN
            ActivateRelease := FALSE;
            StepRunning := FALSE;
            StepIndex := 6;
        END_IF;
    6: // MoveUp
        MoveUp := TRUE;
        IF UpLimitSwitch THEN
            MoveUp := FALSE;
            StepRunning := FALSE;
            StepIndex := 7;
        END_IF;
    7: // MoveLeft
        MoveLeft := TRUE;
        IF LeftLimitSwitch THEN
            MoveLeft := FALSE;
            StepRunning := FALSE;
            StepIndex := 0; // default loop
        END_IF;
    END_CASE;
ELSE
    // When not running, ensure all commands are off
    MoveDown := FALSE;
    MoveUp := FALSE;
    MoveLeft := FALSE;
    MoveRight := FALSE;
    ActivateGrip := FALSE;
    ActivateRelease := FALSE;
END_IF;

Now we need to handle step index increment after step completion in each mode differently:

- In single step mode: after step completes (StepRunning becomes FALSE), we should not automatically increment StepIndex; we should keep StepIndex at the current step (which is now the completed step). Actually we want to advance to next step on next start press. So we need to increment StepIndex only when start is pressed. In the above code, we automatically set StepIndex to next step after completion. That would cause the next step to start automatically, which is not desired for single step mode. So we need to differentiate.

Thus we need separate logic for each mode for step index management.

Option: Use separate variables for each mode's step index, but that seems redundant.

Better: Use a state variable for each mode that determines whether to automatically advance after completion. For example, we can have a boolean "AutoAdvance". In single cycle and continuous modes, AutoAdvance = TRUE; in single step mode, AutoAdvance = FALSE (since we wait for start). However, we also need to differentiate that in continuous mode we also need to loop after step 7.

Thus we can have:

VAR
    StepIndex : INT := 0;
    StepRunning : BOOL := FALSE;
    AutoAdvance : BOOL := FALSE;
    ContinuousRunning : BOOL := FALSE;
    CycleComplete : BOOL := FALSE;
END_VAR

When we start a cycle (single cycle or continuous), we set AutoAdvance := TRUE. In single step mode, we set AutoAdvance := FALSE.

When StepRunning = TRUE and step completes, we set StepRunning := FALSE and then if AutoAdvance then StepIndex := (StepIndex + 1) mod 8 and also if not ContinuousRunning and StepIndex = 0 (i.e., completed step 7) we may set CycleComplete or stop.

But we also need to handle that in continuous mode we keep looping even after step 7.

Thus we can implement the step completion logic in a common block but with conditions.

Alternatively, we can have separate case blocks for each mode.

Simplify: We'll implement separate logic for each mode. That might be clearer.

We'll have three main branches: IF SingleStepMode THEN ... ELSIF SingleCycleMode THEN ... ELSIF ContinuousMode THEN ... END_IF.

Within each branch, we handle start detection and step execution.

We'll also need to handle the case where the start button is pressed while already running (i.e., ignore). We'll also need to handle resetting step index when starting a new cycle.

We'll also need to ensure that when mode changes, we reset step index and step running.

We'll also need to ensure that we set commands only for the active mode.

Thus we can do:

IF SingleStepMode THEN
    // Single step mode logic
    IF StepRunning THEN
        // execute step and check for completion
        CASE StepIndex OF
        0: MoveDown := TRUE; IF DownLimitSwitch THEN MoveDown := FALSE; StepRunning := FALSE; // do not auto advance
        1: ActivateGrip := TRUE; IF GripLimitSwitch THEN ActivateGrip := FALSE; StepRunning := FALSE;
        2: MoveUp := TRUE; IF UpLimitSwitch THEN MoveUp := FALSE; StepRunning := FALSE;
        3: MoveRight := TRUE; IF RightLimitSwitch THEN MoveRight := FALSE; StepRunning := FALSE;
        4: MoveDown := TRUE; IF DownLimitSwitch THEN MoveDown := FALSE; StepRunning := FALSE;
        5: ActivateRelease := TRUE; IF ReleaseLimitSwitch THEN ActivateRelease := FALSE; StepRunning := FALSE;
        6: MoveUp := TRUE; IF UpLimitSwitch THEN MoveUp := FALSE; StepRunning := FALSE;
        7: MoveLeft := TRUE; IF LeftLimitSwitch THEN MoveLeft := FALSE; StepRunning := FALSE;
        END_CASE;
        // after completion, we stay at same step index (i.e., do not increment)
        // next start press will increment step index before starting next step.
    ELSE
        // waiting for start
        IF StartRisingEdge THEN
            // start the current step
            StepRunning := TRUE;
        END_IF;
        // ensure commands off
        MoveDown := FALSE; MoveUp := FALSE; MoveLeft := FALSE; MoveRight := FALSE;
        ActivateGrip := FALSE; ActivateRelease := FALSE;
